# Shell

The default shell is PowerShell (a Git Bash tool also exists, with POSIX syntax). Use the syntax of the tool you call.

- In PowerShell: no `export`, no bash heredoc `<<EOF`, no `&&` assumptions in Windows PowerShell 5 (PowerShell 7 has them), and quote refs such as `@{u}`.
- Pass multi-line text to a native command with a single-quoted here-string whose closing `'@` is at column 0.
- Use absolute paths for targets of `rm` and `mv`.
- For long `gh` bodies use a UTF-8 file and `--body-file`.
- `just` recipes run in PowerShell on Windows (`set windows-shell` in `justfile`) and in `sh` elsewhere. Keep recipes to simple commands that work in both.
- In the Git Bash tool: a backtick inside a double-quoted string or an unquoted heredoc runs a command, so text that holds backticks (Markdown, Rust doc comments) goes into a file written by the editor, never into a command line. An argument that starts with `/` is turned into a Windows path, so a criterion filter does not start with one.
- In the Git Bash tool a heredoc halves the backslashes of its text, quoted or not. A Python script passed that way that writes LaTeX turns its `\\times` into `\times`, which Python reads as a tab and "imes": 17 formulas of the design were broken that way in #173 and went to `main`. Text with backslashes goes into a script file written by the editor; after any scripted edit of the design, search the file for control characters (`.cursor/rules/dev-docs.mdc`).
  - #187 broke one formula in each design file the same way, with the sentence above already here: the search was left for later and not made. A script that writes text into a file checks that text for control characters before it writes, and stops when it finds one; the check is part of the edit, not a step after it.
  - A command typed to the Git Bash tool loses backslashes the same way. In #208, `sed 's/\./\\./g'` typed to the tool printed `0.1.0`, and the same line run from a file printed `0\.1\.0`. An escape is tested from a file written with the editor.
- `python3` on this machine is the Microsoft Store stub, which prints "Python" and exits 49. A CI script that calls `python3` runs here with a directory in front of `PATH` that holds a `python3` script calling Python by its absolute path. That directory is written `/c/...` in `PATH`: `C:/...` is split at its colon and not searched (#208).
- A script that edits a source file replaces text it expects exactly once and stops when the count differs. Files here have CRLF or LF line ends: normalize before matching and write the same ends back.
  - A file changed for a while (a counter, a clock) is saved aside as a copy of its bytes and copied back, not read and written again by a script. In #190 two files restored by Python were left with LF ends, a difference of line ends only, which `git status` showed before a commit.
- A script that puts a new item into a source file puts it before the doc comment of the item it comes before, not before the item's first line of code: in #133 a function went between another function and its doc comment, which then described the wrong one (the compiler says nothing).
  - #197 did it again, with the sentence above already here: `interval_inside` went between `eval` and its doc comment, and the review found it. After the insertion the script looks at the lines just before the new item and just after it, and stops when a doc comment (`///`) runs into an item it was not written for.
- A script that replaces a span finds its start and its end each by a marker that occurs once, checked as for one replacement; a marker found by `index` alone is the first of several. In #197 the end of a span was a loop that a function earlier in the file had too, and part of the file came out twice (put back from the commit).
- A script is not edited by another script: an edit applied twice left a duplicated line in M3-4. Change the script with the editor and run it again.
- `gh pr checks --watch` does not return when the PR merges meanwhile: run it under `timeout`.
- The Git Bash tool rewrites an argument that starts with `/` into a Windows path, so an MSVC tool called from it (`dumpbin /nologo /disasm`) reads its options as files and writes nothing useful. Call such tools from PowerShell. In #194 a disassembly came out as two lines.
